\section{Conclusion and Future Work}
In this paper, we proposed an approach to automatically generate questions based on the provided context. Our method uses a combination of natural language processing (NLP) techniques and machine learning algorithms to identify relevant entities and relationships within the given text. We evaluated our approach on a dataset of question-answering pairs and achieved promising results.

However, there are several limitations to our current implementation. For example, our system relies heavily on the quality of the input data and may struggle with noisy or ambiguous text. Additionally, our approach focuses primarily on extracting entities and relationships, and does not consider other important aspects of question generation such as syntax, semantics, and pragmatics.

To address these limitations, we plan to explore several avenues for future research. One potential direction involves incorporating additional NLP techniques, such as named entity recognition (NER) and part-of-speech tagging, to improve the accuracy of our entity extraction module. Another possible approach involves using machine learning models that can capture more complex patterns and relationships within the input text, such as graph neural networks or transformer-based architectures.

Ultimately, our goal is to develop a robust and flexible question generation system that can be applied to a wide range of domains and applications. By continuing to refine and expand our approach, we hope to make significant contributions to the field of natural language processing and artificial intelligence.

